<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place</p><h1 class="enc-title">Delagoa Bay</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Maputo Bay</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Delagoa Bay, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maputo">Maputo</a> Bay, is a large natural harbour on the coast of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a>. From the 16th century Portuguese, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>, Austrian and British traders competed for its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> trade with the Tembe and other local chiefdoms. Its trade affected the rise of the Mthethwa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boer</a> republics later sought it as their outlet to the sea.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/delagoa-bay.jpg" alt="Delagoa Bay" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Image Science and Analysis Laboratory, NASA-Johnson Space Center · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Delagoa_bay.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
